  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 343 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nSouthwest flow aloft will be in place across the region Friday...as \nupper level ridging remains anchored across the Southern Plains and \na deep upper low across the northern Rockies. Friday looks to remain \nmostly dry across the region as the upper level ridge tries to nose \nnorthward across the plains. There will be a weak front located \nacross the area, but little in the way of upper forcing will be a \nfactor in any convection that may otherwise develop near the \nboundary.\n\nConditions are trending wetter for the weekend. Saturday, the \nnorthern Rockies upper low will slowly track east-southeastward and \nbe entering western Wyoming by late in the day. Rather moist \neasterly low-level flow should aid in the the development of late \nafternoon convection across the western High Plains...as better \nascent begins to overspread the area with the approaching low. It \nappears that upscale growth will occur Saturday night as the showers \nand storms track eastward across the area. The overall severe threat \nseems low, but early on there appears to be at least some potential \nacross the Panhandle.  \n\nSunday looks to remain unsettled. The upper low will very slowly \ntrack across Wyoming, with continued favorable dynamics for at least \nscattered showers and a few thunderstorms downstream across our \narea. The low will slowly fill as it moves across South Dakota \nMonday. Still could be at least some shower/thunder potential \nthrough the day. \n\nBy Tuesday, another upper trough moves onshore the west coast. An \narea of sub-tropical moisture will be located across the desert \nsouthwest into Mexico. This appears to be pulled northeastward \nacross the area as the southwest upper flow strengthens ahead of the \nwest coast trough. This could spell more rain chances Tuesday into \nTuesday night.",
